{
  "result": "There is a specific hour in the morning, just before the sun decides to commit, when the world feels unfinished. The sky is not quite blue, not quite gray. The air holds its breath. I love that hour, even though I rarely wake up for it on purpose. \n\nIt\u2019s the hour when cities are still honest. Delivery trucks rumble down empty streets. A baker pulls the first tray of bread from the oven, and the smell spills out onto the sidewalk for no one in particular. A cat sits perfectly still on a fence, watching a sparrow that hasn\u2019t noticed it yet. Everything is potential. \n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be finished products. We want to be the person who has figured it out \u2014 the career, the relationship, the five-year plan laminated and hanging on the wall. But that unfinished morning hour reminds me that we are allowed to be drafts. \n\nI think of my own life as a desk covered in scattered notes. Some ideas are scribbled out. Some are underlined three times. Some pages are blank, waiting. And that\u2019s okay. The most interesting books are the ones with wide margins where the reader has argued with the author. \n\nSo if you find yourself feeling incomplete, remember: the day hasn\u2019t started yet either. You are both arriving, together, in that soft, uncertain light. And there is a quiet freedom in not being done. Take your time; unfinished things can still be beautiful and enough.",
